    <entry>
        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Rewrite Digital audio concepts guide]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/46103</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/46103"/>
        <updated>2026-10-12T11:47:48.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55398995?u=9f6a9837c9879eceb0abde487a338b92d4e206fc&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><p dir="auto">Follow up from <a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5788626366" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/46088" data-hovercard-type="pull_request" data-hovercard-url="/mdn/content/pull/46088/hovercard" href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/46088">#46088</a>.</p>
<p dir="auto">The Digital audio concepts guide is needlessly verbose and repeats the same point multiple times (it looks like multiple people went in and added their own self-contained paragraphs without caring about what's already written). It also sketches over important bits about how audio sampling and digitization work. Most importantly the "Psychoacoustics" section has many factually incorrect bits, or at least bits that don't please my linguist's brain. I've rewritten it to IMO be more technically precise and also has a better leadup on the basics of acoustic physics.</p>]]></content>
